I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.records._grounding_kernel. Its documented rule says, "The grounding verdict as the harness gives it, with no relaxation and the value-in-span check on." The two reply texts are:

Reply A: {"value": "a", "span": "a b", "document": "a b"}

Reply B: {"value": "a", "span": "a  b", "document": "a b"}

The rule requires GROUNDED for Reply A, because the span "a b" occurs verbatim in the document "a b" and the value "a" is in the span. The rule requires SPAN_NOT_IN_DOCUMENT for Reply B, because the span "a  b" (with two spaces) does not occur verbatim in the document "a b" (with one space), and no relaxation is allowed. But the function returns GROUNDED for both: its span check calls _span_occurs with relaxations=(), yet _span_occurs normalises whitespace before checking, so "a  b" becomes "a b" and matches the document. This makes the two replies indistinguishable to the check. The conjecture is false if _grounding_kernel returns SPAN_NOT_IN_DOCUMENT for Reply B, or if the documented rule is read as permitting whitespace normalisation even when no relaxation is specified.